388	\subsection{Setup}
389	
390	\textbf{Datasets and tasks.} Six LTSF benchmarks (ETTh1/h2/m1/m2~\citep{zhou2021informer}, Weather, Electricity~\citep{wu2021autoformer}) spanning hourly to 15-minute sampling and $7$--$321$ channels, with chronological splits and channel-wise normalization~\citep{nie2023patchtst}. We evaluate forecasting at $H{=}96$ primary (multi-horizon in Appendix~\ref{app:horizon}) and $20\%$-masked imputation.
391	
392	\textbf{Backbones.} Five backbones spanning three normalization regimes: (i)~learnable-affine RevIN-inside-encoder MOMENT-small/large~\citep{goswami2024moment} (internal RevIN~\citep{kim2021revin}); (ii)~Moirai~\citep{woo2024moirai} and Moirai-MoE, which use RMSNorm internally with only non-learnable per-instance I/O scaling (no learnable-affine RevIN inside the encoder pipeline); (iii)~Chronos~\citep{ansari2024chronos} (T5-style, no instance normalization) and Timer-XL~\citep{liu2025timerxl} (LayerNorm only, no instance normalization) as no-instance-normalization negative controls (per-backbone normalization specifications in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}, \S\ref{sec:cross_backbone}). The collapse mechanism predicts that only regime~(i) is vulnerable: routing signal is destroyed by RevIN's learnable-affine normalization upstream of the encoder, whereas non-learnable I/O scaling preserves the (M,$\Sigma$) statistics in the hidden states the router reads. MOMENT-small is primary. Primary results use a strictly frozen backbone; freeze-level ablations follow.
393	
394	\textbf{Baselines.} Four tiers, kept separate to avoid confounding frozen-vs-unfrozen and adapter-vs-from-scratch comparisons: \emph{frozen-adapter} (three fixed adapters; $5$-expert independent ensemble; AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix}); \emph{frozen PEFT} (LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora}, $108$-run sweep, App.~\ref{app:lora_sweep}; TRACE~\citep{li2025trace}); \emph{unfrozen} (full fine-tuning, all blocks, best of $5$ heads $\times 2$ LRs; extended schedules in App.~\ref{app:extended_ft}); \emph{from-scratch calibration} (DLinear~\citep{zeng2023dlinear}).
395	
396	\textbf{Training and statistics.} Adam ($\eta{=}10^{-3}$), MSE loss, $15$ epochs, batch $128$; $5$ seeds for the core grid, $3$ for ablations. Significance via Wilcoxon signed-rank, Bonferroni-corrected over 6 datasets $\times$ 5 seeds ($^{*}\!/^{**}\!/^{***}$ for $p{<}0.05/0.01/0.001$). Full hyperparameters in App.~\ref{app:setup}; code and a \texttt{verify.py} script re-deriving every numerical claim will be released on acceptance.
397	
398	\subsection{Main Results}
399	\label{sec:main_results}
400	
401	RR-MoA's case rests on three claims: that fixed-topology adapters are suboptimal, that the natural MoE remedy collapses on instance-normalized backbones, and that the collapse cannot be recovered by standard rescue mechanisms. We establish each in turn before comparing RR-MoA against the seven-baseline suite (fixed adapters, LoRA, TRACE, independent ensembles, AdaMix, full fine-tuning, DLinear).
402	
403	{\looseness=-1 \textbf{RR-MoA vs.\ fixed adapters.} Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa} compares Top-2 RR-MoA against the best fixed adapter, using the \emph{same five expert heads} in all rows. Across the primary 6-dataset LTSF grid $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds $=$ \textbf{54 configurations}, RR-MoA wins all 54 with tight standard deviations.}
404	
405	\begin{table}[!htbp]
406	\centering
407	\caption{\textbf{Freeze-level ablation} (test MSE, mean$\pm$std, 3 seeds $\{42,43,44\}$, H=96). RR-MoA (sparse Top-2 routing over 5 experts) vs.\ the best single-head adapter from $\{$linear, attention, conv$\}$ trained with the same protocol. ``MSE $\Delta\%$ vs.\ single-head'' is the relative MSE change of RR-MoA over the single-head baseline; negative $=$ lower MSE $=$ better. RR-MoA wins \textbf{27/27} paired (cell, seed) comparisons in this table; combined with Appendix~\ref{app:extended_rrmoa} the score is \textbf{54/54} across 6 datasets $\times$ 3 freeze levels $\times$ 3 seeds. The gain is largest when frozen (``Frozen Paradox'').}
408	\label{tab:rrmoa}
409	\small
410	\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
411	\toprule
412	Dataset & Freeze level & RR-MoA & Best single-head & MSE $\Delta\%$ vs.\ single-head \\
413	\midrule
414	\multirow{3}{*}{ETTh1}
415	 & Frozen (0/8)   & $\mathbf{0.690 \pm 0.021}$ & $1.241 \pm 0.015$ & $\mathbf{-44.4\%}$ \\
416	 & Last-2 (2/8)   & $\mathbf{0.727 \pm 0.074}$ & $1.030 \pm 0.139$ & $-29.4\%$ \\
417	 & Last-4 (4/8)   & $\mathbf{0.749 \pm 0.036}$ & $1.101 \pm 0.120$ & $-31.9\%$ \\
418	\midrule
419	\multirow{3}{*}{ETTm1}
420	 & Frozen (0/8)   & $\mathbf{0.571 \pm 0.073}$ & $1.115 \pm 0.075$ & $\mathbf{-48.7\%}$ \\
421	 & Last-2 (2/8)   & $\mathbf{0.623 \pm 0.032}$ & $0.891 \pm 0.049$ & $-30.1\%$ \\
422	 & Last-4 (4/8)   & $\mathbf{0.571 \pm 0.034}$ & $0.866 \pm 0.016$ & $-34.1\%$ \\
423	\midrule
424	\multirow{3}{*}{Weather}
425	 & Frozen (0/8)   & $\mathbf{0.289 \pm 0.008}$ & $0.530 \pm 0.020$ & $-45.5\%$ \\
426	 & Last-2 (2/8)   & $\mathbf{0.251 \pm 0.005}$ & $0.478 \pm 0.025$ & $-47.4\%$ \\
427	 & Last-4 (4/8)   & $\mathbf{0.256 \pm 0.014}$ & $0.497 \pm 0.033$ & $\mathbf{-48.4\%}$ \\
428	\bottomrule
429	\end{tabular}
430	\end{table}
431	
432	\begin{table}[!htbp]
433	\centering
434	\caption{\textbf{AdaMix routing collapse} (entropy max ${\approx}1.609$). Under unfreezing, entropy ${\to}0.000$. \colorbox{green!8}{Green}: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers entropy ($0.66$--$1.32$), proving RevIN is the causal mechanism. Extended grid in Appendix~\ref{app:extended_rrmoa}.}
435	\label{tab:adamix}
436	\small
437	\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
438	\toprule
439	Dataset & Freeze level & MSE (mean$\pm$std) & Routing entropy (mean$\pm$std) \\
440	\midrule
441	\multirow{4}{*}{ETTh1}
442	 & Frozen  & $1.105 \pm 0.026$ & $0.629 \pm 0.436$ \\
443	 & Last-2  & $1.153 \pm 0.000$ & $\mathbf{0.000 \pm 0.000}$ \\
444	 & Last-4  & $1.154 \pm 0.001$ & $\mathbf{0.000 \pm 0.000}$ \\
445	 & \cellcolor{green!8} Last-4 (no RevIN) & \cellcolor{green!8} $0.575 \pm 0.075$ & \cellcolor{green!8} $\mathbf{1.315 \pm 0.148}$ \\
446	\midrule
447	\multirow{4}{*}{ETTm1}
448	 & Frozen  & $1.008 \pm 0.012$ & $0.487 \pm 0.371$ \\
449	 & Last-2  & $1.061 \pm 0.088$ & $0.218 \pm 0.309$ \\
450	 & Last-4  & $1.123 \pm 0.000$ & $\mathbf{0.000 \pm 0.000}$ \\
451	 & \cellcolor{green!8} Last-4 (no RevIN) & \cellcolor{green!8} $0.426 \pm 0.072$ & \cellcolor{green!8} $\mathbf{1.103 \pm 0.376}$ \\
452	\midrule
453	\multirow{4}{*}{Weather}
454	 & Frozen  & $0.459 \pm 0.017$ & $0.509 \pm 0.307$ \\
455	 & Last-2  & $0.607 \pm 0.002$ & $\mathbf{0.000 \pm 0.000}$ \\
456	 & Last-4  & $0.607 \pm 0.002$ & $\mathbf{0.000 \pm 0.000}$ \\
457	 & \cellcolor{green!8} Last-4 (no RevIN) & \cellcolor{green!8} $0.232 \pm 0.045$ & \cellcolor{green!8} $\mathbf{0.661 \pm 0.501}$ \\
458	\bottomrule
459	\end{tabular}
460	\end{table}
461	
462	{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why does standard MoE routing fail?} To understand the source of RR-MoA's advantage, we examine AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix} adapted to a learned hidden-state softmax router, an established recipe in MoE-adapter and sparsely-gated MoE work (e.g.,~\citealp{wang2022adamix,fedus2022switch}; Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}). Table~\ref{tab:adamix} shows that AdaMix collapses to a single expert whenever backbone layers are unfrozen: entropy reaches $0.000\pm0.000$ in 7/12 unfrozen configurations; the remaining 5 stay below $0.55$ (vs.\ max ${\approx}1.61$) with high cross-seed variance ($\sigma$ up to $0.71$), indicating stochastic collapse rather than stable partial routing. The green rows reveal the cause: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers entropy from $0.000$ to $0.66$--$1.32$, proving that normalization is the causal mechanism (on the ETTh1 last-4 setting we tested, BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm produce the same collapse; Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}). The causal contrast is sharpest when comparing backbones directly (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}): under identical last-4 unfreezing on identical data, MOMENT entropy crashes to $0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps while Timer-XL (which lacks RevIN) stays at $1.60$ for all $400$ steps, opposite trajectories from the same code.}
463	
464	\begin{figure}[!htbp]
465	\centering
466	\includegraphics[width=0.60\columnwidth]{figures/causal_contrast.pdf}
467	\vspace{-6pt}
468	\caption{\textbf{Causal contrast: MOMENT vs Timer-XL} (ETTh1, last-4 unfreezing). Solid: unfrozen; MOMENT entropy collapses $0.51{\to}0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps; Timer-XL holds ${\approx}1.60$ for $400$ steps. Dashed: frozen controls. Dotted: $\log K{=}1.609$ ceiling. Same code, same data, only normalization differs. Per-step entropy and per-expert gradient-norm trajectories are in Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}).}
469	\label{fig:causal_contrast}
470	\end{figure}
471	
472	In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) and wins \textbf{54/54} configurations, with MSE improvements of $26\%$ to $79\%$ over the best fixed adapter. Notably, on Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, where the stripped statistics carry little information), RR-MoA does not improve over fixed adapters, consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction that low $R$ implies little benefit (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}).
473	
474	{\looseness=-1 \textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} The trajectory and causal-contrast figures formalize into a sharp prediction: if normalization-driven gradient co-adaptation is the collapse mechanism, then within a fixed MoE architecture, frozen should match or beat unfrozen. Empirically, frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on all 6 LTSF datasets (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}; the three-dataset subset in Table~\ref{tab:baselines} is consistent). The two-factor headline gap (frozen+MoE vs.\ unfrozen+single-head) decomposes into:
475	\emph{(M1) MoE architectural advantage (dominant, $26$--$79\%$):} the best fixed adapter loses to RR-MoA \emph{at the same freeze level} (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}), isolating M1 from M2.
476	\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze effect (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional):} within RR-MoA itself, frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two; SR-MoA independently replicates the $4/6$ pattern (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} attributes M2 to gradient co-adaptation: the Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (unfreezes backbone but cuts routing-weighted feedback) degrades MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), and disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on normalization rather than optimization. A $90$-config hyperparameter tune (LR $10^{-5}$--$10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine + warmup, layerwise decay, batch up to $512$) still loses by $51$--$71\%$, consistent with optimization not being the bottleneck within the sweep we ran.}
477	
478	\begin{proposition}[Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse]
479	\label{prop:frozen}
480	Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), and $\dot{p}$ amplifies whichever expert currently has lower loss, creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ removes the co-adaptation feedback through the backbone (full non-collapse additionally requires $(\ell_1{-}\ell_2)$ to flip sign across input regimes, i.e., expert specialization, which is satisfied by the topologically distinct RR-MoA pool). Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
481	\end{proposition}
482	
483	{\looseness=-1 \textit{Remark.} Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} covers linear $A\mathbf{x}$; Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b confirms it in the full 8-block Transformer within ${\sim}50$ steps. Independent ensembles are $37$--$46\%$ worse (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}): learned routing, not mere diversity, drives gains.}
484	
485	\textbf{Rescue-baseline sweep.} To rule out that the collapse is merely under-tuned, we sweep five standard MoE rescue families (Switch load-balance~\citep{fedus2022switch}, ST-MoE z-loss~\citep{zoph2022stmoe}, entropy regularization, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{wang2025remoe}, and expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) across eleven new rescue configurations plus the load-balance baseline already in Table~\ref{tab:adamix} ($12$ configurations total $\times$ 6 datasets $\times$ 2 freeze levels $\times$ 5 seeds $= 720$ run-cells) (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}, Appendix~\ref{app:rescue_details}). The best rescue (z-loss $c_z{=}0.01$) reduces MSE by only $10.9\%$, still $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA. Excessive balancing ($\alpha{=}10.0$) actively hurts MSE ($+67.7\%$). This is consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when routing information is structurally absent from the hidden states, none of the standard rescue mechanisms we tested can compensate.
486	
487	\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic.} The diagnosis predicts the same fix should work for any router that reads raw input. We validate this with three router-level controls. \textbf{SR-MoA} (Self-Routed MoA, Figure~\ref{fig:srmoa_arch}; following the router-restructuring direction of~\citealp{lv2025aoe,liu2026routingfree,cheng2025ermoe}) drops the external router entirely: each expert has its own sigmoid gate $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$. SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (per-(dataset, backbone) cell range over the $252$-run, six-backbone grid; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed} top vs.\ Tables~\ref{tab:rrmoa},~\ref{tab:rrmoa_extended} frozen rows) and replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Gating the same SR-MoA architecture on hidden states degrades by $+87$--$768\%$ on all 6 datasets ($p{=}0.016$); on Timer-XL (no RevIN) the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$, an order-of-magnitude smaller effect on the three datasets where both backbones were tested (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}), consistent with the effect being normalization-induced. \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}) swaps AdaMix's hidden-state router for a raw-input router and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}, Figure~\ref{fig:residual_ia_arch}) extends the principle to the expert level via a shared NLinear raw branch + gated backbone residual, closing the DLinear gap. Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls} summarizes all eight controls.

