{\looseness=-1 \textbf{Why does standard MoE routing fail?} To understand the source of RR-MoA's advantage, we examine AdaMix~\citep{wang2022adamix} adapted to a learned hidden-state softmax router, an established recipe in MoE-adapter and sparsely-gated MoE work (e.g.,~\citealp{wang2022adamix,fedus2022switch}; Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}). Table~\ref{tab:adamix} shows that AdaMix collapses to a single expert whenever backbone layers are unfrozen: entropy reaches $0.000\pm0.000$ in 7/12 unfrozen configurations; the remaining 5 stay below $0.55$ (vs.\ max ${\approx}1.61$) with high cross-seed variance ($\sigma$ up to $0.71$), indicating stochastic collapse rather than stable partial routing. The green rows reveal the cause: disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers entropy from $0.000$ to $0.66$--$1.32$, proving that normalization is the causal mechanism (on the ETTh1 last-4 setting we tested, BatchNorm1d and GroupNorm produce the same collapse; Appendix~\ref{app:norm_generalization}). The causal contrast is sharpest when comparing backbones directly (Figure~\ref{fig:causal_contrast}): under identical last-4 unfreezing on identical data, MOMENT entropy crashes to $0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps while Timer-XL (which lacks RevIN) stays at $1.60$ for all $400$ steps, opposite trajectories from the same code.}

\begin{figure}[!htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.60\columnwidth]{figures/causal_contrast.pdf}
\vspace{-6pt}
\caption{\textbf{Causal contrast: MOMENT vs Timer-XL} (ETTh1, last-4 unfreezing). Solid: unfrozen; MOMENT entropy collapses $0.51{\to}0.000$ in ${\sim}40$ steps; Timer-XL holds ${\approx}1.60$ for $400$ steps. Dashed: frozen controls. Dotted: $\log K{=}1.609$ ceiling. Same code, same data, only normalization differs. Per-step entropy and per-expert gradient-norm trajectories are in Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}).}
\label{fig:causal_contrast}
\end{figure}

In contrast, \textbf{RR-MoA} maintains healthy routing entropy ($1.0$--$1.57$) and wins \textbf{54/54} configurations, with MSE improvements of $26\%$ to $79\%$ over the best fixed adapter. Notably, on Traffic ($R{=}0.14$, where the stripped statistics carry little information), RR-MoA does not improve over fixed adapters, consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}'s prediction that low $R$ implies little benefit (Figure~\ref{fig:signal_ratio}).

{\looseness=-1 \textbf{The Frozen Paradox.} The trajectory and causal-contrast figures formalize into a sharp prediction: if normalization-driven gradient co-adaptation is the collapse mechanism, then within a fixed MoE architecture, frozen should match or beat unfrozen. Empirically, frozen RR-MoA beats \emph{full fine-tuning} (all 8 blocks unfrozen, best of 5 heads $\times$ 2 LRs) by $12$--$79\%$ on all 6 LTSF datasets (Figure~\ref{fig:frozen_paradox}; the three-dataset subset in Table~\ref{tab:baselines} is consistent). The two-factor headline gap (frozen+MoE vs.\ unfrozen+single-head) decomposes into:
\emph{(M1) MoE architectural advantage (dominant, $26$--$79\%$):} the best fixed adapter loses to RR-MoA \emph{at the same freeze level} (Table~\ref{tab:rrmoa}), isolating M1 from M2.
\emph{(M2) Within-architecture freeze effect (smaller, $\leq 13\%$, non-unidirectional):} within RR-MoA itself, frozen is best on $4/6$ datasets; light unfreezing wins by up to $13\%$ on the other two; SR-MoA independently replicates the $4/6$ pattern (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} attributes M2 to gradient co-adaptation: the Router-Detached Gradient Flow ablation (unfreezes backbone but cuts routing-weighted feedback) degrades MSE by $+35$--$100\%$ on three datasets (Appendix~\ref{app:routing_ablations}), and disabling RevIN inside MOMENT recovers full-FT by $54$--$61\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:extended_ft}), pinning the mechanism on normalization rather than optimization. A $90$-config hyperparameter tune (LR $10^{-5}$--$10^{-6}$, $100$ epochs, cosine + warmup, layerwise decay, batch up to $512$) still loses by $51$--$71\%$, consistent with optimization not being the bottleneck within the sweep we ran.}

\begin{proposition}[Gradient Co-Adaptation Drives Entropy Collapse]
\label{prop:frozen}
Consider a $2$-expert MoE with backbone $\mathbf{h}{=}A\mathbf{x}$, router $p{=}\sigma(\mathbf{w}^\top\mathbf{h})$, loss $\ell{=}p\,\ell_1{+}(1{-}p)\,\ell_2$. Under gradient descent on $(A,\mathbf{w})$: \textup{(i)} the dominant expert's loss decreases faster ($\dot{\ell}_k \propto {-}p_k\|\nabla_\mathbf{h}\ell_k\|^2$), and $\dot{p}$ amplifies whichever expert currently has lower loss, creating self-reinforcing collapse toward $p{\in}\{0,1\}$; \textup{(ii)} freezing $A$ removes the co-adaptation feedback through the backbone (full non-collapse additionally requires $(\ell_1{-}\ell_2)$ to flip sign across input regimes, i.e., expert specialization, which is satisfied by the topologically distinct RR-MoA pool). Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}.
\end{proposition}

{\looseness=-1 \textit{Remark.} Proposition~\ref{prop:frozen} covers linear $A\mathbf{x}$; Figure~\ref{fig:trajectory}b confirms it in the full 8-block Transformer within ${\sim}50$ steps. Independent ensembles are $37$--$46\%$ worse (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}): learned routing, not mere diversity, drives gains.}

\textbf{Rescue-baseline sweep.} To rule out that the collapse is merely under-tuned, we sweep five standard MoE rescue families (Switch load-balance~\citep{fedus2022switch}, ST-MoE z-loss~\citep{zoph2022stmoe}, entropy regularization, ReMoE ReLU routing~\citep{wang2025remoe}, and expert-choice routing~\citep{zhou2022expertchoice}) across eleven new rescue configurations plus the load-balance baseline already in Table~\ref{tab:adamix} ($12$ configurations total $\times$ 6 datasets $\times$ 2 freeze levels $\times$ 5 seeds $= 720$ run-cells) (Table~\ref{tab:rescue}, Appendix~\ref{app:rescue_details}). The best rescue (z-loss $c_z{=}0.01$) reduces MSE by only $10.9\%$, still $2.7{\times}$ worse than RR-MoA. Excessive balancing ($\alpha{=}10.0$) actively hurts MSE ($+67.7\%$). This is consistent with Observation~\ref{thm:mi_decomp}(ii): when routing information is structurally absent from the hidden states, none of the standard rescue mechanisms we tested can compensate.

\textbf{The principle is architecture-agnostic.} The diagnosis predicts the same fix should work for any router that reads raw input. We validate this with three router-level controls. \textbf{SR-MoA} (Self-Routed MoA, Figure~\ref{fig:srmoa_arch}; following the router-restructuring direction of~\citealp{lv2025aoe,liu2026routingfree,cheng2025ermoe}) drops the external router entirely: each expert has its own sigmoid gate $\sigma_k(\mathbf{X}_\mathrm{raw})$. SR-MoA \emph{outperforms} RR-MoA by $13$--$42\%$ across all 6 datasets (per-(dataset, backbone) cell range over the $252$-run, six-backbone grid; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed} top vs.\ Tables~\ref{tab:rrmoa},~\ref{tab:rrmoa_extended} frozen rows) and replicates the Frozen Paradox (frozen best on 4/6; Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}). Gating the same SR-MoA architecture on hidden states degrades by $+87$--$768\%$ on all 6 datasets ($p{=}0.016$); on Timer-XL (no RevIN) the same raw-vs-hidden comparison yields only $+4$--$9\%$, an order-of-magnitude smaller effect on the three datasets where both backbones were tested (Table~\ref{tab:self_routed}), consistent with the effect being normalization-induced. \textbf{AdaMix-Raw} (Appendix~\ref{app:adamix_details}) swaps AdaMix's hidden-state router for a raw-input router and recovers entropy to $1.41$--$1.57$ and MSE by $41$--$88\%$ on all 12 cells. \textbf{Residual-IA\textsuperscript{+}} (Appendix~\ref{app:gate_pathology}, Figure~\ref{fig:residual_ia_arch}) extends the principle to the expert level via a shared NLinear raw branch + gated backbone residual, closing the DLinear gap. Table~\ref{tab:causal_controls} summarizes all eight controls.

